<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Fourier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jean-Baptiste Joseph Fourier, Fourier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Baptiste Joseph Fourier was a French mathematician and physicist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>, best known for initiating the study of Fourier series and their use in problems of heat transfer and vibrations. Orphaned at nine, he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and was briefly imprisoned during the Terror. He accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> on the Egyptian expedition in 1798 as secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institut-degypte">Institut d'Égypte</a>, and in 1801 was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">Prefect</a> of Isère in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>, where he began his experiments on the propagation of heat. He is also generally credited with the discovery of the greenhouse effect.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-fourier.jpg" alt="Joseph Fourier" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julien-Léopold Boilly · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fourier2_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
